"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[90077],{290077:e=>{e.exports="# Data Type Mapping\n\nRedshift type → DBSQL type mapping. Includes precision notes and behavioral differences.\n\n## REDSHIFT\n\n### Types with behavioral differences\n\n| Redshift                                    | DBSQL                        | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| ------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `VARCHAR(n)` / `VARCHAR`                    | `STRING`                     | Redshift `VARCHAR` has a max of 65,535 bytes; DBSQL `STRING` has no length cap. Safe to drop the `(n)` in most cases. If the schema must preserve the constraint, use `VARCHAR(n)` — see note below.                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `CHAR(n)` / `NCHAR(n)`                      | `STRING`                     | **CHAR trailing-space drift**: Redshift `CHAR(n)` pads values with spaces and preserves them in comparisons. DBSQL `STRING` does not pad. If the source relies on CHAR padding for equality, `RTRIM` the column on migration or rewrite comparisons as `RTRIM(c) = 'x'`.                                                                                                                             |\n| `BPCHAR`                                    | `STRING`                     | Redshift internal alias for `CHAR(256)`; same trailing-space caveats as `CHAR`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| `TEXT`                                      | `STRING`                     | Redshift treats `TEXT` as `VARCHAR(256)`                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `BOOLEAN`                                   | `BOOLEAN`                    | Identical                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `SMALLINT` / `INT2`                         | `SMALLINT`                   | Identical; Redshift aliases                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| `INTEGER` / `INT` / `INT4`                  | `INT`                        | Identical; Redshift aliases                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| `BIGINT` / `INT8`                           | `BIGINT`                     | Identical; Redshift aliases                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| `DECIMAL(p,s)` / `NUMERIC(p,s)`             | `DECIMAL(p,s)`               | Identical; see precision-drift note below                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `REAL` / `FLOAT4`                           | `FLOAT`                      | 4-byte                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| `DOUBLE PRECISION` / `FLOAT8` / `FLOAT`     | `DOUBLE`                     | 8-byte                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| `DATE`                                      | `DATE`                       | Identical                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `TIME` / `TIMETZ`                           | `STRING`                     | DBSQL has no native TIME type. Store as `HH:MM:SS` string; cast to `TIMESTAMP` for arithmetic.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| `TIMESTAMP` / `TIMESTAMP WITHOUT TIME ZONE` | `TIMESTAMP_NTZ`              | Redshift `TIMESTAMP` is timezone-naive. Map to `TIMESTAMP_NTZ` to preserve naive semantics. Use plain `TIMESTAMP` only when the app has a single-timezone convention and has been validated to tolerate DBSQL's session-TZ display behavior.                                                                                                                                                         |\n| `TIMESTAMPTZ` / `TIMESTAMP WITH TIME ZONE`  | `TIMESTAMP`                  | Redshift `TIMESTAMPTZ` normalizes to UTC on store; DBSQL `TIMESTAMP` is timezone-aware on display (session TZ). Semantics match for the common UTC-storage case.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| `INTERVAL`                                  | `INTERVAL`                   | Both dialects support `INTERVAL` literals (e.g. `INTERVAL '1 day'`). DBSQL allows unit-qualified intervals (`INTERVAL 1 DAY`); either form works.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| `SUPER`                                     | `VARIANT`                    | Redshift's semi-structured `SUPER` type → DBSQL `VARIANT`. Both support path navigation; see `03-query-and-dml.md` for access-operator mapping.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| `HLLSKETCH`                                 | `BINARY` + `HLL_*` functions | Redshift HLL sketches have no direct DBSQL type. Redshift serializes sketches as JSON (sparse) or Base64 (dense); both are Redshift-specific and **not wire-compatible** with DBSQL's `HLL_SKETCH_*` family. Rebuild sketches from raw data on ingest — see _HLL function mapping_ below. If raw data is unavailable, use Redshift-emitted scalar cardinality estimates as a best-effort substitute. |\n| `GEOMETRY`                                  | `GEOMETRY(srid)`             | Native type available (DBR 17.1+, preview). Both dialects use WKT/WKB and `ST_*` functions. Not supported on Iceberg tables. Fallback: store as `STRING` (WKT) and use `ST_GEOMFROMTEXT` to read.                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| `GEOGRAPHY`                                 | `GEOGRAPHY(4326)`            | Native type available (DBR 17.1+, preview). WGS84 (SRID 4326) in both dialects. Not supported on Iceberg tables. Fallback: `STRING` + `ST_GEOGFROMTEXT`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `VARBYTE` / `VARBINARY` / `BINARY VARYING`  | `BINARY`                     | Redshift `VARBYTE(n)` accepts `n` from 1 to 16,777,216 bytes (default 64,000); DBSQL `BINARY` has no practical cap.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n\n### Identical types (no translation)\n\n`INT`, `BIGINT`, `SMALLINT`, `DECIMAL(p,s)`, `NUMERIC(p,s)`, `BOOLEAN`, `DATE`, `FLOAT`, `DOUBLE`, `BINARY` — map 1:1 when the Redshift source uses the standard name. (Redshift's `SUPER` maps to DBSQL `VARIANT` — see the row above.)\n\n### VARCHAR(n) — preserve vs. drop the length\n\nDBSQL accepts `VARCHAR(n)` as a constrained `STRING`. When:\n\n- Migrating a schema **faithfully** (the downstream app or CHECK constraints rely on max length): keep `VARCHAR(n)`.\n- Migrating for **flexibility** and the length was never enforced by app logic: drop `(n)` and use plain `STRING`.\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\ncol VARCHAR(100)\n\n-- DBSQL (constrained — same max length as Redshift)\ncol VARCHAR(100)\n\n-- DBSQL (unconstrained — Databricks-native default)\ncol STRING\n```\n\nRedshift byte-counting note: Redshift `VARCHAR(n)` counts **bytes**, and multi-byte characters consume more than one `n`. DBSQL `VARCHAR(n)` counts **characters**. If the source schema was sized for byte counts and stored non-ASCII text, the DBSQL character count may accept strings that Redshift would have truncated — a silent safety improvement, but may need review for exact-round-trip schemas.\n\n### CHAR(n) — trailing-space pad drift (CRITICAL)\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift CHAR(5): 'ab' is stored as 'ab   ' (3 trailing spaces)\n-- Redshift equality pads the constant too:\nSELECT 1 WHERE c = 'ab';   -- TRUE on a CHAR(5) column\n\n-- DBSQL STRING: stored without padding unless loaded with pad\nSELECT 1 WHERE c = 'ab';   -- FALSE if value is 'ab   '\n```\n\nThree remediation options, from most targeted to most schema-wide:\n\n1. **`RTRIM` at the comparison site** — `WHERE RTRIM(c) = 'ab'`. Cheapest, but easy to miss when the CHAR column is referenced in many queries.\n2. **`COLLATE UTF8_BINARY_RTRIM`** — DBSQL's native trailing-space-insensitive collation. Apply to the column in DDL: `CREATE TABLE t (c STRING COLLATE UTF8_BINARY_RTRIM);`. All equality/inequality comparisons on `c` then ignore trailing spaces, matching Redshift CHAR semantics without per-query rewrites.\n3. **Strip on load** — `RTRIM` values during ingest. Use when the pad was an artifact of CHAR storage and downstream consumers never depend on it.\n\n### SUPER → VARIANT\n\nRedshift `SUPER` → DBSQL `VARIANT`. Path access operator: Redshift dot (`s.a.b`) → DBSQL colon (`s:a.b`). See `03-query-and-dml.md` for the full mapping.\n\n### Decimal precision\n\nDBSQL arithmetic on DECIMAL can produce different precision results due to promotion rules:\n\n```sql\n-- Division increases precision; if combined precision exceeds 38, rounding occurs.\n-- Workaround: cast to smaller precision before operations.\nSELECT CAST(a AS DECIMAL(18,4)) / CAST(b AS DECIMAL(18,4));\n```\n\nRedshift's precision promotion rules are similar but not identical. If the source migration must preserve exact arithmetic results, add explicit casts.\n\n### `::` type cast\n\nNative DBSQL operator with identical semantics — leave unchanged unless rewriting for clarity:\n\n```sql\n-- Both Redshift and DBSQL\nSELECT amount::DECIMAL(10,2), CURRENT_DATE::STRING;\n```\n\n### Conversion functions\n\n| Redshift                | DBSQL                                                                                   | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `CAST(expr AS type)`    | `CAST(expr AS type)`                                                                    | Identical                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `expr::type`            | `expr::type`                                                                            | Identical (native in both)                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `CONVERT(type, expr)`   | `CAST(expr AS type)`                                                                    | Redshift supports both `CAST` and Postgres-style `CONVERT`; DBSQL uses `CAST`                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |\n| `TO_NUMBER(str, 'fmt')` | `TRY_CAST(REGEXP_REPLACE(str, '[^0-9.\\-]', '') AS DECIMAL(p,s))`                        | DBSQL has no format-string numeric parsing. Strip formatting characters (currency, thousands separators, whitespace) then cast. Use `CAST` instead of `TRY_CAST` if the source had no defensive handling.                                                                                             |\n| `TO_CHAR(num, 'fmt')`   | `FORMAT_NUMBER(num, n)` — decimals only; `FORMAT_STRING('%,.2f', num)` — `printf`-style | Neither dialect fully replicates Postgres numeric format patterns (`'9,999.00'`, `'$FM999,999.00'`, etc.). `FORMAT_NUMBER` covers `N` decimal places with thousands commas; `FORMAT_STRING` handles width / padding / sign. For currency prefixes, concatenate: `CONCAT('$', FORMAT_NUMBER(num, 2))`. |\n\n### HLL function mapping\n\nRedshift HLL functions operate on `HLLSKETCH` (Redshift-specific binary). DBSQL's `HLL_SKETCH_*` family is **wire-incompatible** — sketches cannot be deserialized across dialects; rebuild from raw data:\n\n| Redshift                           | DBSQL                         | Notes                                        |\n| ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `HLL(col)`                         | `APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT(col)`  | Single-call cardinality (no sketch retained) |\n| `HLL_CREATE_SKETCH(col)`           | `HLL_SKETCH_AGG(col)`         | Aggregate raw values into a sketch           |\n| `HLL_COMBINE(s1, s2)`              | `HLL_UNION(s1, s2)`           | Combine two sketches scalar-to-scalar        |\n| `HLL_COMBINE_SKETCHES(sketch_col)` | `HLL_UNION_AGG(sketch_col)`   | Aggregate-combine sketches across rows       |\n| `HLL_CARDINALITY(sketch)`          | `HLL_SKETCH_ESTIMATE(sketch)` | Extract estimated distinct count             |\n\nStored Redshift sketches cannot be passed to DBSQL — rebuild every sketch from raw data on ingest using `HLL_SKETCH_AGG`.\n"}}]);
